==De minimis==
{{CRT shortcut|DM|China}}

<!--T:138-->
Although there is no explicit ''de minimis'' or incidental use provision in China's Copyright Law, a case in the Beijing Internet Court provided its interpretation of Article 24(2), which allows "appropriate quotation" of published works for purposes such as introducing, commenting on a work, or illustrating a point, to rule that the use of a painting as a prop in a TV drama without authorization constitutes appropriate quotation and does not infringe copyright.

<!--T:139-->
The Beijing Internet Court analyzed the case under Article 24(2) and considered the following factors:

<!--T:140-->
* Purpose of Use: The painting was employed to illustrate the character's fondness for cats, not to reproduce and publish the original artwork itself.

<!--T:141-->
* Extent of Use: The painting occupied a small portion of the screen (ranging from 0.3% to 4% of the frame) and was not the focus of any scene.

<!--T:142-->
* Impact on the Copyright Owner's Rights: The low-resolution, incidental appearance of the painting did not substitute for the original work or harm its commercial potential.

<!--T:143-->
Moreover, the court ruled that due to the constraints of the TV medium, omitting the author's name did not constitute an infringement of the plaintiff's right to attribution. <ref name=China-deminimis/>

<!--T:144-->
A similar approach was taken in a 2004 [[s:zh:北京市第一中级人民法院（2003）一中民初字第11687号民事判决书|case]] heard by the Beijing No. 1 Intermediate People's Court, where the defendant used a song in a TV drama without obtaining license from the composer or the plaintiff. However, the use lasted only about seven seconds, involving two short lines of lyrics and the corresponding melody. The court found that the use did not adversely affect the normal exploitation of the work or substantially harm the rights holder's legitimate interests. The court therefore held that the defendant's conduct did not constitute copyright infringement.

==Freedom of panorama== <!--T:36-->

</translate><noinclude>{{CRT shortcut|FOP|China}}
</noinclude><translate><!--T:148--> {{nutshell|title=This section|language=en|China generally has freedom of panorama for architecture and permanently installed 3D public artworks, including commercial use, but not for prominently depicted 2D works such as murals or outdoor paintings. Court decisions have interpreted the law narrowly for 2D artworks, making uploads of such works risky unless they appear [[Commons:De minimis|only incidentally]] in the image. Temporary public displays are also generally not covered.}}</translate><translate>
<!--T:55-->
In general, {{OkMark}} ''', with attribution''' {{tlx|FoP-China}}, for outdoor and indoor works (since June 2021 with the 2020 amendment of the copyright law allowing indoor works<tvar name="ref"><ref name=Law2020/></tvar>) but {{warnsign|practically may be problematic}} with regards to images of 2D works like murals and permanent outdoor paintings (reference: <tvar name="2">[[Commons:Deletion requests/File:Mao Zedong portrait.jpg]]</tvar>). To summarize:
*{{OkMark}} for architecture,
*{{OkMark}} for 3D works like sculptures (and possibly things like handicrafts, ceramics, and tiles), and
*generally {{NotMark}} for 2D works like murals and outdoor paintings, unless their presences in images are not being main subjects.

<!--T:37-->
The reproduction of artistic, architectural, or applied artwork, is covered under the Copyright Law of the People's Republic of China, which allows reproduction of works in an outdoor public place if the author and the name of the original work is attributed.

<!--T:38-->
*Article 24: In the following cases, a work may be exploited without the permission from, and without payment of remuneration to, the copyright owner, provided that the name or designation of the author and the title of the work are mentioned and the normal use of the work, or unreasonably damage the lawful rights and interests of the copyright owner shall not be affected:<br>(10) copying, drawing, photographing, or video recording of an artistic work located or on display in a public place;...<br>The provisions of the preceding paragraph shall apply to restrictions on copyright-related rights.

<!--T:39-->
The "Copyright Law of the People's Republic of China" (2020) Article 24, clause 10 states that:
:''<nowiki>[</nowiki>A<nowiki>]</nowiki> work may be exploited without the permission from, and without payment of remuneration to, the copyright owner, provided that the name or designation of the author and the title of the work are mentioned and the normal use of the work, or unreasonably damage the lawful rights and interests of the copyright owner shall not be affected:''
:''copying, drawing, photographing, or video recording of an artistic work located or on display in a public place''

<!--T:40-->
Regulations and court decisions regarding to freedom of panorama:
* {{zh icon}} '''''[[:s:zh:最高人民法院关于审理著作权民事纠纷案件适用法律若干问题的解释|Interpretation of the Supreme People's Court on Several Issues Concerning the Application of Law in the Trial of Cases of Civil Disputes over Copyright]]''''', Article 16: Artistic works stipulated in Article 24, Paragraph 1, Item 10 of the [[:s:zh:中华人民共和国著作权法|''Copyright Law'']] refer to sculptures, paintings, calligraphy and other works of art that are set up or displayed in public places.<br />Copying, painting, photographing, and video recording of the artistic works specified in the preceding paragraph may be used again in a reasonable manner and scope in accordance with the law, provided that, without the permission of the copyright owner, they are not set up, displayed, or publicly disseminated in the same manner as the original works.
</translate>
<translate>
<!--T:59-->
* {{zh icon}} '''''[[:s:zh:最高人民法院关于对山东省高级人民法院《关于山东天笠广告有限责任公司与青岛海信通信有限公司侵犯著作权纠纷一案的请示报告》的复函|Reply on the "Report on the Request for Infringement of Copyright Disputes between Shandong Tianyi Advertising Co., Ltd. and Qingdao Hisense Communications Co., Ltd." of the Shandong Provincial Higher People's Court, the Supreme People's Court]]:''''' (Dispute on using [[:Category:May Fourth Square|May Wind]] sculpture in advertisements) ... Here, for 'reasonable approach and scope', the 'approach and scope' for profit purposes should be included. This is the original intention of formulating the ''[[:s:zh:最高人民法院关于审理著作权民事纠纷案件适用法律若干问题的解释|Judicial Interpretation]]''. This provision of judicial interpretation is in conformity with the basic spirit of fair use as stipulated by the Berne Convention, and it is also in line with the legislation of most countries in the world."
</translate>
<translate>
<!--T:41-->
* {{zh icon}} '''''[[:s:zh:王巨贤与绍兴市水利局其他著作权权属侵权纠纷审判监督民事判决书|Wang Juxian vs. Shaoxing Water Conservancy Bureau on Other Copyright Ownership Infringement Dispute Trial Supervision, The Supreme People's Court]]''''': As mentioned above, China's Copyright Law exempts the public from the obligation to attribute the author's name and the name of the work while exempting the author from the obligation to authorize and pay for the copying of the sculpture in the outdoor public place. Under normal circumstances, the public can only rely on the annotation of the outdoor art work itself to confirm the author's name and the name of the work without any obligation to verify.

